{
  "concept": "Three easy-to-draw principal rays from a point on the object all converge at one point after a converging lens, and that crossing point locates and sizes the real, inverted image predicted by the thin-lens equation.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal optical axis spanning the full width of the figure",
    "Biconvex lens at center, pale blue fill with darker blue outline, tall and narrow",
    "Vertical black dashed line through the lens center (principal plane / center line)",
    "Red upward arrow to the left of the lens representing the object, standing on the axis",
    "Double-headed vertical arrow at far left labeled h_o measuring object height",
    "Red dot on the axis to the left of the lens labeled F (near focal point)",
    "Red dot on the axis to the right of the lens labeled F (far focal point)",
    "Horizontal double-headed bracket from lens center to right focal point labeled f",
    "Ray 1 (red): from object tip parallel to axis, refracts at lens, passes through far focal point, continues down-right past the image",
    "Ray 2 (green): from object tip straight through lens center undeviated, continues down-right",
    "Ray 3 (purple): from object tip through the near focal point, refracts at lens, emerges parallel to the axis to the right",
    "Small numeric ray labels '1' (red), '2' (green), '3' (purple) placed near each incoming ray with arrowheads showing direction of travel",
    "Short red downward arrow below the axis on the right at the ray crossing point = inverted real image",
    "Double-headed vertical arrow labeled h_i measuring image height below the axis",
    "Horizontal double-headed bracket below the axis from object to lens labeled d_o",
    "Horizontal double-headed bracket below the axis from lens to image labeled d_i",
    "Arrowheads mid-ray indicating propagation direction on each colored ray",
    "Optional readout panel showing d_o, d_i, f, magnification m = -d_i/d_o, and image type (real/inverted vs virtual/upright)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "objectDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Moving the object changes where the rays cross: as d_o decreases toward f the image races away from the lens and grows, and inside f the rays diverge and the image becomes virtual and upright — the lens equation 1/d_o + 1/d_i = 1/f made visible.",
      "label": "Object distance d_o (in units of f)",
      "range": [0.4, 5.0, 0.05],
      "default": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "raySelect",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Each principal ray obeys one simple rule (parallel-in → through F, straight through the center, through F → parallel-out), yet all three land on the same image point — so any two of them are enough to construct the image."
    },
    {
      "id": "measureOverlay",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "The geometric drawing and the algebraic quantities are the same thing: the brackets for f, d_o, d_i and the arrows for h_o, h_i are exactly the terms in the lens and magnification equations.",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Ray construction", "Measurement overlay"],
      "default": "Ray construction"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The setup",
      "narration": "A red arrow object stands on the optical axis to the left of a converging lens, with focal points F marked one focal length f on each side. Nothing has been drawn yet except the object and the lens.",
      "state": { "objectDistance": 2.5, "measureOverlay": "Ray construction" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Three rays, three rules",
      "narration": "Rays 1, 2 and 3 leave the same point at the object's tip. Click any one of them to isolate it and see its rule highlighted: parallel-then-through-F, straight-through-center, or through-F-then-parallel.",
      "state": { "objectDistance": 2.5, "measureOverlay": "Ray construction" }
    },
    {
      "title": "They agree on one point",
      "narration": "All three refracted rays cross below the axis at a single point. A short downward red arrow there is the real, inverted image — light actually passes through it, so it could be caught on a screen.",
      "state": { "objectDistance": 2.5, "measureOverlay": "Ray construction" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Measuring the drawing",
      "narration": "Switch on the measurement overlay: brackets appear for d_o, d_i and f, and arrows for h_o and h_i. The ratio h_i/h_o equals -d_i/d_o, and the three distances satisfy 1/d_o + 1/d_i = 1/f.",
      "state": { "objectDistance": 2.5, "measureOverlay": "Measurement overlay" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Push the object far away",
      "narration": "With the object far from the lens, the rays converge just beyond the far focal point: d_i shrinks toward f and the image becomes small and strongly reduced.",
      "state": { "objectDistance": 5.0, "measureOverlay": "Measurement overlay" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide it toward the focal point",
      "narration": "As d_o approaches f, the refracted rays become nearly parallel, d_i shoots off to the right and the inverted image grows enormous — the tradeoff between object and image distance in action.",
      "state": { "objectDistance": 1.15, "measureOverlay": "Measurement overlay" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Inside the focal point",
      "narration": "Move the object closer than f and the refracted rays diverge; extending them backward gives an upright, magnified virtual image on the object side — the magnifying-glass case.",
      "state": { "objectDistance": 0.6, "measureOverlay": "Measurement overlay" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Three rays drawn by three different simple rules meet at the same point — that single intersection is the image, so ray tracing alone gives you both where the image is and how big and which way up it is.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape layout. A thin black horizontal optical axis runs across the middle. A tall pale-blue biconvex lens with a darker blue outline sits slightly right of center, with a black vertical dashed line through its axis of symmetry. The object is a thick red upward arrow standing on the axis well to the left; a thin black double-headed arrow at the far left margin labels its height h_o. Two small red dots sit on the axis, one a focal length left of the lens and one a focal length right, each labeled F in black below the axis; a black horizontal bracket with arrowheads from the lens center to the right-hand F is labeled f. Three rays emanate from the object tip: ray 1 red, horizontal until the lens then sloping down through the right focal point and continuing off the lower right; ray 2 green, a straight line from tip through the lens center continuing down-right; ray 3 purple, sloping down through the left focal point, reaching the lens and then running horizontally to the right below the axis. Small bold colored numerals 1, 2, 3 sit just above their respective incoming rays with mid-ray arrowheads. The three refracted rays intersect below the axis right of the lens where a short thick red downward arrow marks the inverted image, with a thin black double-headed arrow beside it labeled h_i. Below the axis, black double-headed brackets span object-to-lens (d_o) and lens-to-image (d_i). When the overlay toggle is off, only the rays, lens, object, image and F dots are visible; when on, all brackets, height arrows and a small numeric readout of d_o, d_i, f and m appear. Highlighted rays on click render at full opacity and thicker stroke while the other two dim to light gray, and the relevant marker (near F, lens center dot, or far F) is emphasized with a ring. In the virtual-image regime the diverging refracted rays get dashed backward extensions to an upright red image arrow on the object side, and the d_i bracket is drawn on the left and annotated as negative."
}

